WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.435 --> 00:00:08.850
PATRICK KLING: Hi, my
name is Patrick Kling,

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.520
and I'm an engineering manager
on the health care and life

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.160
sciences team here
at Google Cloud.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.430
Our mission is to organize the
world's health care information

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.670
and to make it accessible,
secure, and useful,

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.400
to improve the health and health
care of people in communities

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.810
globally.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.850
Today we're going to
talk about our offering

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.950
in the medical imaging space.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.690
First we'll hear
from Omer Khawar,

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.570
who is the VP of
engineering at IDgital.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.140
His company built a innovative
teleradiology solution

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.343
on top of Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:39.343 --> 00:00:41.010
Afterwards, we'll
hear from my colleague

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.280
Sasha Sicular, who will be
walking us through the cloud

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.655
architecture of this system.

00:00:45.655 --> 00:00:47.280
And then finally,
I'll be back up here,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.405
and I'll be talking about
the platform underpinning

00:00:49.405 --> 00:00:51.910
all of this, which is the
Cloud Health Care API.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.890
And with that, I'll
hand it over to Omer.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.560
OMER KHAWAR: Thanks, Patrick.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:56.410
Hi, everybody.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:57.190
Thanks for coming.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.400
I know it's the last
day and people probably

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.650
have flights and things that
they want to get back to,

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.620
so I appreciate the
people that did come.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.340
We have a lot of exciting
things to talk about today.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.960
At IDgital, our mission
is to, essentially,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:16.650
deliver better patient outcomes
through the application

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.750
of advanced technology.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.280
When our founders sat down,
they looked at the entire health

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.840
care sector and determined that
the area of the industry that

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.440
was most ripe for
this kind of benefit

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:39.590
was, in fact, radiology, and
specifically, teleradiology.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.230
So they thought
about the problems

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.640
that they were trying to solve.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.860
There are over
35,000 radiologists.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.470
Less than 3% of
them are networked.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:56.420
There's no common platform
and a decided lack

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.980
of technological advancement.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.240
This market is very large.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:02.930
It's $5 billion.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:07.140
But it's a highly
fragmented mix of solutions.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:13.380
And so what we're running
into is essentially

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.950
two major areas of challenge.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:21.450
In the market itself, we've got
radiologist burnout happening.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.280
And there's a couple of factors
that are feeding into this.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.180
One is that
radiologists are being

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.840
asked to read more and more,
so the volumes are increasing.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.470
Also, radiologists are
being asked to do it faster.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.180
Oftentimes, there are
patient expectations.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.480
You know, I'd like
my reading back fast.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.360
There are also clinical
concerns if there

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.420
are emergent indications
that things need

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.180
to be turned around rapidly.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.790
So while the volume's
going up and people

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.040
are asked to do
their work faster,

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.840
the technology hasn't
been able to keep up.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.150
In addition, another
trend that's worrying

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.600
is that coming out of
medical programs we're

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:10.030
having fewer and fewer people
actually opt to do radiology.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:13.810
So all these factors
are driving radiologists

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:17.660
individually to try to do a lot,
lot more on the same platforms

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.660
and systems that they've
been using for decades.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:28.450
We looked at some market surveys
and found that satisfaction

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:31.090
amongst the users-- and
user is the radiologist--

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.720
is down at the 40% level.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.790
And for a $5 billion market
to be down that low--

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.670
and just to be clear,
that 40% satisfaction

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.950
is with any of the
solutions that exist.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.875
It wasn't their
particular solution.

00:03:43.875 --> 00:03:46.000
So they're essentially
saying there's nothing there

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.340
that really helps me execute
my work rapidly and achieve

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.250
those patient outcomes
that we're looking for.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:04:00.970
And finally, with the trend in
fewer and fewer radiologists

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:02.650
and the increasing
cost of health care

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:05.380
that everybody is
aware of, we're

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:08.950
finding that we get very
poor rural coverage.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:11.050
And institutionally,
also, there are

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:16.360
challenges in delivering
radiological care.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:21.279
On the technology side,
we've got a lot of problems.

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:23.440
We've got cumbersome
data exchange.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.530
So with any technology
that has been around

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.420
for a few decades,
what we're finding is

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.420
we've got a lot of
different systems.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:34.085
They all speak a
little bit differently,

00:04:34.085 --> 00:04:36.460
and so we've got all of these
point-to-point integrations

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:38.230
going on.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:40.060
And that's in the
radiology workflow.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.530
So from end to
end, you know, they

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:43.420
could touch four or
five different systems

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.270
provided by four or
five different vendors.

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.400
And as those vendors have
software that gets released,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.830
we essentially have problems
in those integrations.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:55.810
That's leading to
low reliability.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:59.037
So it is not
uncommon-- and people

00:04:59.037 --> 00:05:01.120
that are not in the field
may be shocked by this--

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.130
but it's not uncommon
for radiologists

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:06.730
to essentially show up to
work and not be able to work.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:11.680
Their platform that allows
them to do teleradiology

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.020
just is not available
for hours at a time.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.010
And finally, low innovation--
so I mentioned that before.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:22.340
With the legacy systems that
are in place and the technology

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:24.170
that exists, what
we're finding is

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:27.380
it's very hard to get some of
the cutting edge technology,

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:30.650
specifically machine
learning models

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:33.620
and artificial
intelligence to be applied.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.150
People are working very hard--

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:39.620
some of you are in the
room-- on these technologies.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:41.510
And they're running
into real challenges

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:46.430
in delivering that into
the workflow for radiology.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:50.290
I mean, I was talking to one
vendor that has to actually--

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:52.040
I mean, they're a team
of data scientists,

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:56.150
and they have to take the VMs
and put them in sites in order

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:57.590
to deliver that.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:04.820
This is really-- these high road
blocks are causing people just

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:07.020
to not to use the technology.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.550
And then finally, scalability--

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:11.480
so all of the things I talked
about from a fragmentation

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:17.870
perspective, from a
technology perspective,

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.610
are leading to an
inability to scale

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.860
any of the individual solutions
that are on the market

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:24.240
today, across the country.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:32.050
So our solution--
so what we looked at

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:37.390
was to create a cloud native,
zero footprint teleradiology

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:39.790
solution.

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:44.520
The entire solution is built on
the cloud, including the DICOM

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:45.020
store.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.860
So for those of you
not in the industry,

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:52.090
that's essentially the images
from the various modalities.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:55.730
So those are on the cloud,
which is, believe it or not,

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.885
a significant step.

00:06:57.885 --> 00:06:59.260
There's an embedded
DICOM viewer,

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.210
so that also is in the cloud.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:06.220
And so that's important to
reduce the multiple vendor

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:09.130
approach that
currently exists today.

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:10.960
And then both RIS
and PACS capabilities

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.920
are built into the platform.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.000
From a machine learning and AI
support perspective, you know,

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.410
the step one was to
create a platform

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:22.530
and then start to
drive in the capability

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:28.080
to add machine learning and
AI from cloud solutions that

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.820
exist today.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:35.040
Embedded voice support is
an important work enabler

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:36.660
for radiologists.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.060
And then streamlined
integration with facilities--

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.160
so those of you that
don't work in the space

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.610
may not understand how difficult
it is to actually connect

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.610
to facilities, whether that's
a hospital or an urgent care

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:51.400
center in some rural area.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.460
But after we went live,
as VP of engineering,

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:55.870
those are all my problems.

00:07:55.870 --> 00:08:00.170
And so it was great to get some
of the experience on our team,

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:04.860
you know, decades of experience,
doing this at the beginning,

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.530
so that we could build into
our solution capabilities that

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.285
actually make that faster.

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:15.370
It seems like the
simplest thing to do,

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:18.900
but it's actually been the
hardest to achieve in practice.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.120
So now I'll talk about
the platform itself.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.450
So those capabilities
can be broken down

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:28.420
into technical building blocks.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:30.170
Later on, we'll go
through this in detail.

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.330
So I'm not going to spend too
much time on this slide going

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:33.205
through every bullet.

00:08:33.205 --> 00:08:36.110
But I do think it's
important to kind of have

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.320
some context for the
capabilities that are there.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:45.210
Data ingest-- so as I said,
many, many, many points.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:46.350
This is all HIPAA data.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:48.780
So it contains
patient information.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:51.130
The images themselves
need to be secure.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:53.820
So we needed to make sure it
was both secure and scalable,

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.100
scalable to thousands of points.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.800
And so you'll see that in
our solution today, later on.

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:03.700
The platform itself--
so this, essentially,

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:04.720
is the work management.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:06.820
It's the workhorse
of the platform.

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.520
And that's doing all of the
management of the work that's

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:12.640
coming in, assigning that
out to radiologists, managing

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:16.060
access, all of those things that
you would expect from a work

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:18.550
manager.

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:22.780
This is also where we
would introduce clinical,

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.310
AI based decision making.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:29.620
The image viewer-- it's
already on the cloud.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:30.940
Our images are in the cloud.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:33.350
We have very low
latency in image fetch,

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:34.892
which again, is
a challenge based

00:09:34.892 --> 00:09:36.100
on what I've described today.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:42.250
And messaging-- so again,
we'll talk about this in detail

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:44.200
why we went this
route, but it was

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.810
important to have the
scalability within the system

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:50.890
to pass information
back and forth.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:53.260
And mobile-- so I
think I referred

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:55.630
to earlier kind of
a lack of access

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:57.650
from an institutional
perspective.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:01.120
So we made a mobile tool set
that allows, essentially,

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:04.630
mobile radiology to be enabled.

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:07.870
Essentially, these are
technicians that drive around

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:10.510
with a mobile unit, and
they can visit institutions

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:14.620
such as jails or hospitals,
elder care centers,

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:19.680
and our platform enables that
workflow also, seamlessly.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.200
And finally, machine learning--

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:27.000
so that's really, I think-- the
point of making a new platform

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.590
is to enable that.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:31.360
And we'll talk in detail
about how we did that

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.795
and what the capabilities
are to essentially create

00:10:34.795 --> 00:10:36.170
that continuous
loop of learning.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:42.790
And then underneath
this all, a data store.

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:45.760
So it was important that
the data be very close

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:47.050
to the application.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:50.230
The latency that
users experience

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.630
today is very much
related to where

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:54.460
the data is, where
are the images,

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:55.970
where is the patient data.

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:57.720
And so it was very
important to bring that

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:01.210
close and on the same platform.

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:04.600
So why did we pick
Google for this solution?

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.460
Well, as you can see
on the left side,

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:10.840
all cloud services provide
a set of capabilities.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:13.870
And so what distinguished
Google for us was really

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:20.380
that they had created and were
in alpha with a HIPAA compliant

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:21.310
data store.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:22.990
And that was the
crux of the decision.

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:28.630
Also, the seamless
scalability that we

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:33.310
get with Kubernetes,
multi-region networking,

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:37.780
and then the rapid ML tools that
some of you have seen today,

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:43.210
and additions to those that were
announced at the conference.

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:45.820
So what does that mean from
an engineering perspective?

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:48.910
Well, it meant that I
could work with our design

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:53.260
teams, our SMEs in the
space, and essentially

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:55.960
use these tool sets and
accelerate the delivery

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:57.190
of the platform itself.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.290
I don't need to sit there
and redesign everything

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:00.490
from scratch.

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:03.640
My engineers don't need to go
in and make all of these things.

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:06.970
I can leverage the services and
capabilities that Google has,

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:09.220
and later on we'll talk about
specifically which ones.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.660
So what did we learn
working with Google?

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:19.400
Well, we found them to
be a very active partner.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:22.850
As we went and
designed our solution,

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:24.830
there was very much a
conversation with them

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:26.990
about the approach
we would take,

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:30.230
how we would solve various
problems we ran into,

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:35.010
and I can't speak enough
about the health care team.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:38.580
They were awesome
throughout the process.

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:43.610
In addition, we worked with
Zazmic, our engineering

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:47.525
partner, and they were critical,
too, in getting to market fast.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.350
So again, some of
the cloud services

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:55.180
I mentioned that accelerated
our time to market--

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:58.070
and we'll go through
those in detail.

00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:01.400
So technical lessons learned--

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:03.500
going through this process
of making this platform

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:05.470
from scratch,
cloud native, there

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.262
were a couple of things
we learned that are

00:13:07.262 --> 00:13:08.320
pertinent in health care.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:12.140
So number one, resource
matching was a key activity.

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:14.000
So what do I mean by that?

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:16.700
Essentially, as we
found these pieces

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:19.520
of data coming from
various sources,

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:22.220
getting that data associated
with both the patient

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:24.920
and the order that was
going on was critical,

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:26.850
and to be able to
do that at scale.

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:29.630
So we're talking
petabytes of information

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:34.550
that needs to hook together
and be related at the point

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:38.720
that the radiology is trying
to perform their tasks.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.090
Association of DICOM
based studies with orders

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:42.600
has many edge cases.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.890
So those of you that have
tried to use this standard

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:49.640
will understand the
standard is not standard.

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:55.140
And every vendor has
interpreted it their way.

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:58.600
And then finally, scaling--

00:13:58.600 --> 00:13:59.890
we needed to scale.

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:01.600
We needed to scale fast.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:04.540
And we needed to scale without
affecting our production

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:06.430
clients.

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:10.330
So we solve this in the
first case with microservices

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:13.950
in Pub/Sub, and
in the second case

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:17.460
by introducing and accommodating
the flexibility that

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:22.980
exists in DICOM, and
on the third with GKE.

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:25.950
And we're looking at
Istio and Service Mesh.

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:29.880
There was an announcement at
the conference on Service Mesh

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:31.350
also that we're
very interested in.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:34.650
So we'll continue to work with
Google and with our engineering

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:36.840
partners to achieve those.

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:39.090
So next, Sasha is going
to come up and talk

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:41.578
about the architecture
in detail.

00:14:41.578 --> 00:14:42.870
SASHA SICULAR: Thank you, Omer.

00:14:46.357 --> 00:14:47.690
Appreciate everybody being here.

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:49.800
I'm very grateful
to be on this stage

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:53.130
and talking to you all about
this very interesting solution.

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:54.450
A little bit about myself--

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:57.510
before joining Google, I was
director of medical informatics

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:01.320
at Columbia University for many,
many, many years, many more

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:03.940
years than I'd like to admit.

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:06.600
And it's from that
background and learning

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:08.730
that I have an
appreciation for many

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:11.730
of these technical challenges
that organizations are trying

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:15.810
to solve, large and
small, ultimately to drive

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:18.370
better patient outcomes.

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:20.370
So what we're going
to do today is

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:22.440
talk through the
specific architecture

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:28.320
that we put in
place with IDgital

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:34.830
to help facilitate this zero
on prem footprint, entirely

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:35.790
cloud-based solution.

00:15:39.450 --> 00:15:42.830
I'll just highlight a couple
of the steps right now.

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:45.470
Some of the key things
that I think everybody

00:15:45.470 --> 00:15:47.030
should be focused on--

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:47.800
networking.

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:51.960
So networking is critically
important, of course.

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:55.728
As soon as you start talking
about moving to the cloud,

00:15:55.728 --> 00:15:57.270
how are you going
to facilitate that?

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:59.525
So the network connectivity
is absolutely critical.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:04.140
From that, we moved
deeper into the stack.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:08.870
We talk about how Google
Cloud Platform organizes

00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:14.270
its internal structures around
the concept of a project.

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:20.480
MLLP and DICOM, those are
two critical legacy protocols

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:23.030
that are ubiquitous in
the health care space,

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:29.570
and any organization looking to
move into this kind of method

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:32.570
of working in the cloud
needs to understand

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:35.600
the limitations of
both MLLP and DICOM

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:38.060
and how you can overcome
those limitations.

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:41.313
And we'll talk about that
in a little bit more detail.

00:16:41.313 --> 00:16:43.980
From there, I want to point out,
obviously, the health care API.

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:46.970
The health care API is
really pivotal and central

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:49.370
to the entire conversation
of the architecture, what

00:16:49.370 --> 00:16:54.230
it allows you to do, the kind
of capabilities it provides,

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:57.370
the kind of systems and services
you can build around it.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:59.000
And then ultimately,
the health care

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:00.680
API will go into
even further detail

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:05.415
with Patrick, when he
speaks in the third act.

00:17:05.415 --> 00:17:08.339
You know, Omer picked up
on something very quickly,

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:13.700
which was, I like to think
of it as liberating data.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:17.500
So right now, to facilitate
day-to-day transactional

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:21.339
operations between
providers and patients,

00:17:21.339 --> 00:17:23.950
those data systems are siloed.

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:28.099
They're isolated within
the on prem environment.

00:17:28.099 --> 00:17:30.620
As we move into the future,
the question becomes,

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:34.680
how do we leverage that data
to provide better service?

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:36.950
Again, ultimately driving
better patient outcomes,

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:40.760
servicing more patients,
and really meeting

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:43.610
the challenges that all
organizations are facing today

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:44.510
in health care.

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:47.090
So by bringing the
data to the cloud

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:50.810
you get to take advantage of
all kinds of functionality

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.732
that's present in the cloud.

00:17:52.732 --> 00:17:54.440
In particular, we'll
talk about BigQuery.

00:17:57.030 --> 00:18:00.570
Underpinning the whole exercise,
of course, is security.

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:03.900
How do you ensure that
you are in compliance

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:11.460
with very stringent
HIPPA regulation regime?

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:15.120
So obviously, HIPPA is the
law of the land in the United

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:16.170
States.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:18.580
Of course, we have
global presence.

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:23.220
So we'll talk about HIPPA today,
but this same conversation

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:25.980
plays internationally.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:29.400
All kinds of jurisdictions are
introducing data protection

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:34.800
related regulation, in general
and also in health care.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.410
So as an organization
that's looking

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:40.298
to take advantage of these
products and functionalities,

00:18:40.298 --> 00:18:42.090
you obviously need to
make sure that you're

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:45.060
doing it in a very secure
and regulated fashion.

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:52.050
So let's talk about the
network connectivity.

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:53.880
What are we trying to
do here, ultimately?

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:57.000
We're looking to bring
data from where it's

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:00.300
being generated into the cloud.

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:02.500
And in order to
do that, you have

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:04.450
to have good network
connectivity.

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:07.570
If you think about
teleradiology,

00:19:07.570 --> 00:19:11.840
the way that those providers
access this information

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:14.060
is that they have to get
that information from where

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:14.762
it's stored.

00:19:14.762 --> 00:19:16.220
And traditionally,
that information

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:21.410
is stored on premises
where it's been generated.

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:24.280
So think about all of the
network connectivity issues

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:29.463
that a specific physical
facility may have.

00:19:29.463 --> 00:19:30.880
Now let's turn it
around and think

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:34.870
about what the networking
opportunities and capabilities

00:19:34.870 --> 00:19:37.690
are of Google.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:44.160
So we have a major, major
networking presence globally,

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:48.690
with tremendous options
up and down the stack,

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:51.990
specifically dedicated
to networking.

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:54.900
Particularly, in
this solution, we're

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:57.850
looking at our
cloud VPN solution.

00:19:57.850 --> 00:19:59.340
And this is what
really enables you

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:03.510
to do a fully zero
footprint installation.

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:07.740
Ultimately, the key thing
that the physical facility

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:11.460
needs to enable is
the VPN termination.

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:14.550
If they can enable that VPN
termination in their networking

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:20.460
stack connected to its
endpoint in the cloud,

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:23.460
that's basically
all you have to do.

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:25.130
Yes, there are other solutions.

00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:27.770
You can look at various
interconnect solutions

00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:29.780
for higher degrees of bandwidth.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:36.860
You can look at even point
to point secure tunnel.

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:39.180
Those are a little bit
higher up the stack.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:43.130
You go from zero footprint
to maybe 99% no footprint.

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:48.120
So cloud VPN was really
are our first intent

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:52.101
and our first interest to
facilitate this technology.

00:20:54.810 --> 00:21:00.210
The key consideration
here is that the VPN

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:05.910
will route the traffic to
the appropriate IP address.

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:08.670
Not just externally,
but also internally.

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:11.460
As we continue the conversation
we look at Kubernetes,

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:14.890
et cetera, we'll
see how all those IP

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:16.140
considerations come into play.

00:21:19.630 --> 00:21:23.570
Project-- so I spent
a little bit of time

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:25.770
earlier talking about this.

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:30.330
In Google Cloud Platform,
one of the major areas

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:34.470
of abstraction in the system
is what we call the project.

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:36.810
And for any of you who have
worked with Google Cloud

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:40.530
Platform previously, you'll
be familiar with this concept.

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:44.250
The project is where you
put all the things, so all

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:48.310
of your networking resources,
all of your virtual machines,

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:51.630
your Kubernetes clusters,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:55.830
They all exist
inside of projects.

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:58.270
And why should we
consider projects

00:21:58.270 --> 00:22:01.120
as a major boundary
for the architecture

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:02.770
of the application?

00:22:02.770 --> 00:22:11.610
Data isolation-- obviously, as
a technical services provider,

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:14.740
IDgital is looking to
onboard many, many customers.

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:18.340
We want to ensure that their
data is completely isolated

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:22.320
from any of the other customers
in the system, specifically

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:26.730
the hard data, the
DICOM images themselves.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:30.875
Also, the project is
where billing happens.

00:22:30.875 --> 00:22:32.250
So when you create
a project, you

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:34.620
create an associated
billing account,

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:36.720
and all of the resources
and consumption

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.000
of those resources
within that project

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:41.170
are then charged into
that billing account.

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:43.470
So for an organization
that's looking

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:46.830
to onboard many,
many customers, you

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:49.830
want to have a very,
very close accounting

00:22:49.830 --> 00:22:54.240
of what those underlying
resources are costing.

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:57.360
So by isolating at
the project level,

00:22:57.360 --> 00:23:00.240
you're able to have a very,
very fine grained control

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:03.630
or insight into what
all of the resources

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:05.700
are costing on a customer
by customer basis.

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:11.610
Again, due to the
fact that the project

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:14.370
is the kind of base
level of abstraction,

00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:16.980
all of the things that are
in the project, health care

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:21.870
API, Google Cloud buckets,
Google Cloud Storage

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:25.770
buckets, virtual
machines, allocation

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:28.030
of reserved external IP
addresses, et cetera,

00:23:28.030 --> 00:23:31.490
et cetera, all of
those items have

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:33.560
quotas associated with them.

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:36.560
So quota management is how
it is handled on a project

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:37.410
by project basis.

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:41.060
So again, this is how
you are able to isolate

00:23:41.060 --> 00:23:44.227
various customers
from clobbering

00:23:44.227 --> 00:23:45.810
other customers, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:51.270
And they maintain their
entire individual ecosystem.

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:52.470
And then finally, security.

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:58.340
Projects, again, not
just data isolation,

00:23:58.340 --> 00:24:00.840
but access to projects
are controlled

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:05.450
via GCPIM, which is identity
and access management,

00:24:05.450 --> 00:24:07.760
and you can very,
very fine grained

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:10.880
control over which systems,
which people, which accounts,

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:15.000
which services can access all
the things within the project.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.180
So for this particular
implementation use

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:21.150
case with IDgital, we have
many projects that play here.

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:26.100
We have production,
development, demo,

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:30.900
where IDgital is able to spin up
a completely identified system

00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:33.600
and demo it to prospective
customers, et cetera.

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:38.650
What you'll see is,
and we'll talk about it

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:41.620
in particular, logging.

00:24:41.620 --> 00:24:46.840
Again, Cloud Audit
logging, Stackdriver,

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:49.330
these are all very
important pieces

00:24:49.330 --> 00:24:54.010
of the overall conversation, and
what we in health care and life

00:24:54.010 --> 00:24:55.780
sciences group
within Google Cloud

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:59.830
recommend as a
HIPAA best practice.

00:24:59.830 --> 00:25:02.680
All of the projects, all
of the independent projects

00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:07.660
in the system, can outbound
their logs to a audit

00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:10.540
and logging dedicated
project that

00:25:10.540 --> 00:25:15.520
can be staffed specifically
by a compliance

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:19.378
organization, separate
group of people, no bleed,

00:25:19.378 --> 00:25:20.170
anything like that.

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:28.410
A project per customer--
talked about that a little bit.

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:33.410
And finally, all of these
pieces can be automated

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:36.220
and this is one of the
pieces of the word scale.

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:40.380
How do you scale the
solution to accommodate,

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:42.850
you know, hopefully you're
going to do good business.

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:46.580
You want to accommodate
lots and lots of customers.

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:49.180
How do you, as an
organization, enable

00:25:49.180 --> 00:25:50.980
the onboarding of all
of these customers,

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:54.190
and managing of all
of these scenarios?

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:55.735
It has to be automated.

00:25:55.735 --> 00:25:57.610
So everything we're
going to talk about today

00:25:57.610 --> 00:26:00.250
can be automated
through Cloud Deployment

00:26:00.250 --> 00:26:03.625
Manager, G Cloud, kube control.

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:12.110
Kubernetes-- Kubernetes
is kind of the base

00:26:12.110 --> 00:26:14.780
layer of scalable
abstraction from

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:16.520
a computational perspective.

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:19.880
In this particular
use case calls

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:24.050
for using Kubernetes to
scale both the MLLP and DICOM

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:29.980
adapters and the application
specific services.

00:26:29.980 --> 00:26:33.130
The key part here
is the networking.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:39.250
So the Kubernetes cluster itself
controls the ingress networking

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:42.370
from your external
on prem customers

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:46.405
to their specific MLLP
and DICOM adapters.

00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:54.070
Of course, everything in
the Kubernetes cluster

00:26:54.070 --> 00:26:58.040
is connected to the entire
logging infrastructure.

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:00.250
And what Kubernetes
really brings to the table

00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:04.980
is, as I said, the ability
to scale the computation.

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:07.550
But on which dimension
do you scale?

00:27:07.550 --> 00:27:11.630
How do you determine
when you should scale?

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:14.942
There is new developments,
of course, always coming,

00:27:14.942 --> 00:27:15.650
new developments.

00:27:15.650 --> 00:27:20.820
So GKE enables you to
scale on custom metrics.

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:24.630
So for example, MLLP is
lightweight from a data

00:27:24.630 --> 00:27:25.320
perspective.

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:27.240
Its high message volume.

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:30.690
DICOM is a little bit heavier
because it's the actual image.

00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:33.630
So they have different
scaling dimensions,

00:27:33.630 --> 00:27:36.840
and by integrating with
custom metrics for scaling,

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:40.730
they can scale independently.

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:43.530
MLLP and DICOM adapters--

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:48.650
this is really a lot of the
meat of the architecture.

00:27:48.650 --> 00:27:55.670
Google publishes open source,
MLLP and DICOM adapters.

00:27:55.670 --> 00:27:58.460
And the way that you
should think about them

00:27:58.460 --> 00:28:01.090
is as a bridge.

00:28:01.090 --> 00:28:08.270
On one side, they receive
legacy MLLP and DICOM protocol

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:09.860
communication.

00:28:09.860 --> 00:28:18.600
On the other side, they egress
health care API protocol.

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:21.440
So this is the real
workhorse here.

00:28:21.440 --> 00:28:24.650
This is what enables
you to bridge

00:28:24.650 --> 00:28:30.650
the legacy on prem environment,
the ubiquitous presence

00:28:30.650 --> 00:28:34.100
of these protocols, and
virtually all hospital oriented

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:39.310
systems around the world to
your cloud-based solution.

00:28:43.332 --> 00:28:45.040
And again, as I
mentioned, these services

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:53.650
are exposed via private internal
load balancer IP addresses.

00:28:53.650 --> 00:28:57.100
Those addresses in turn
are routed through the VPN.

00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:06.500
The Kubernetes cluster
basically plays double duty.

00:29:06.500 --> 00:29:08.870
In addition to hosting the
MLLP and DICOM adapters,

00:29:08.870 --> 00:29:12.000
we're also hosting services.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:17.600
So the classic service oriented
architecture, microservices,

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:22.410
pulling together a specific
set of business logic

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:24.150
to accommodate a
particular business

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:28.100
task into a specific process.

00:29:28.100 --> 00:29:30.920
And these processes
do various things.

00:29:30.920 --> 00:29:34.720
For example in this use
case, we're creating order.

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:37.530
So as messages come
into the system,

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:41.180
they emit notifications
into Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:42.950
We'll talk more about
that in particular.

00:29:42.950 --> 00:29:46.497
And these services listen
for those notifications.

00:29:46.497 --> 00:29:48.080
And when they get
those notifications,

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:49.710
they do specific things.

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:54.290
So for example, they might
listen for a DICOM message

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:58.590
and then create the
associated ORM HL7 message.

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:01.560
They might listen for
the ORM HL7 message

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:05.670
and then create an order in
the transactional Cloud SQL

00:30:05.670 --> 00:30:09.870
database in the IDgital
application itself.

00:30:09.870 --> 00:30:13.690
Resource matching-- Omer
mentioned that briefly.

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:18.900
How do you match an HL7
message to its DICOM study?

00:30:18.900 --> 00:30:20.700
How do you do all these things?

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:22.260
And then finally billing--

00:30:22.260 --> 00:30:24.780
we highlight billing
as well, of course.

00:30:24.780 --> 00:30:27.750
Major component of the system.

00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:29.730
You want to think about
how those bills then

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:34.770
need to be delivered
to those organizations.

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:37.170
Healthcare API.

00:30:37.170 --> 00:30:39.750
All kinds of interesting
things happening here

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:42.090
that Patrick will talk
about in specific detail.

00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:43.590
But some of the
things that I wanted

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:46.290
to highlight in
particular for this use

00:30:46.290 --> 00:30:52.780
case and implementation are the
data store architecture design.

00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:57.590
So we have highlighted
here in the architecture

00:30:57.590 --> 00:30:59.040
three different data stores.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:03.570
One for ORMs and ORUs, so
that's requests for reports

00:31:03.570 --> 00:31:07.380
and then the report reply.

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:15.060
DFTs, Detailed Financial
Transaction HL7 messages.

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:16.830
And then, of course,
DICOM messages.

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:19.620
So ORM and ORU are
in one data store.

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:21.510
DFTs are in another data store.

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:23.970
DICOMs are in their
own data store,

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:27.240
and we had an interesting design
decision to determine, well,

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:30.720
do we need one for real-time
and do we need one for priors?

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:33.000
Or for those not
in the business,

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:37.320
the archive of all of the
previous images that were done.

00:31:37.320 --> 00:31:40.680
And due to how different
microservices--

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:44.470
we've architected various
microservices in the system--

00:31:44.470 --> 00:31:48.560
we decided to put them all
together in one DICOM store.

00:31:48.560 --> 00:31:51.920
Each of those data
stores then output

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:54.620
into their own
Cloud Pub/Sub topic,

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:57.110
and these topics of
course are critical.

00:31:57.110 --> 00:31:59.720
This is where the
microservices listen

00:31:59.720 --> 00:32:01.970
for various pieces
of information, put

00:32:01.970 --> 00:32:06.990
in new pieces of
information, and there

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:09.510
are many ways to interact
with these Pub/Sub stores.

00:32:09.510 --> 00:32:12.900
The takeaway for these is that
it's completely integrated,

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:18.090
so this is completely managed
by Google Cloud Platform.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:19.920
There's all kinds
of capabilities

00:32:19.920 --> 00:32:24.360
to interact with the queues,
to ACK or acknowledge messages

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:26.790
from the queue, or not
acknowledge messages

00:32:26.790 --> 00:32:29.430
from the queue, ensuring
that they get picked up

00:32:29.430 --> 00:32:30.630
at a later date.

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:31.810
Configurable retention.

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:33.965
The messages stay around
until they're processed,

00:32:33.965 --> 00:32:34.840
et cetera, et cetera.

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:38.660
BigQuery.

00:32:38.660 --> 00:32:41.690
This is a really interesting
part of the conversation,

00:32:41.690 --> 00:32:43.810
and I spoke a little bit
about it at the intro.

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:44.560
Omer mentioned it.

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:49.620
Going to the cloud
shouldn't be an apples

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:51.220
to apples conversation.

00:32:51.220 --> 00:32:54.660
It should be an apples to
fruit basket conversation.

00:32:54.660 --> 00:32:57.150
You get all the things
because going into the cloud

00:32:57.150 --> 00:33:01.530
unlocks all of the other
opportunities in the cloud.

00:33:01.530 --> 00:33:03.630
In particular here we're
highlighting BigQuery.

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:09.030
So BigQuery is Google's
data warehouse system.

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:10.640
And once you get
data into BigQuery

00:33:10.640 --> 00:33:12.920
you can do all kinds
of interesting things.

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:14.210
You can enable analytics.

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:16.800
You can enable more
advanced analytics we

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:18.050
love to call machine learning.

00:33:18.050 --> 00:33:20.342
Sprinkle a little bit of
machine learning on everything

00:33:20.342 --> 00:33:22.393
and everything's better.

00:33:22.393 --> 00:33:24.185
But you've got to get
the data there first.

00:33:24.185 --> 00:33:26.810
It has to get there.

00:33:26.810 --> 00:33:29.840
The way that we've designed
the Healthcare API is that it

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:35.710
easily can export data
into BigQuery and cannot

00:33:35.710 --> 00:33:37.660
underestimate easy.

00:33:37.660 --> 00:33:40.030
Easy is incredibly important.

00:33:40.030 --> 00:33:41.565
BigQuery's got all the goodies.

00:33:41.565 --> 00:33:42.490
It's scalable.

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:44.230
It's fully managed.

00:33:44.230 --> 00:33:48.670
Auditable, again, integrates
with external reporting tools,

00:33:48.670 --> 00:33:52.510
like Looker and our Data
Studio, Tableau, accessible

00:33:52.510 --> 00:33:54.760
via standard ANSI SQL.

00:33:54.760 --> 00:33:57.430
So for those of you in the
audience who have a SQL

00:33:57.430 --> 00:34:00.220
background this is fantastic.

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:01.620
eUnity viewer.

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:06.050
The eUnity viewer is
the DICOM viewer itself.

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:08.889
So this is a commercially
available product

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:12.730
from another company.

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:15.610
It integrates directly
with the Healthcare API.

00:34:15.610 --> 00:34:19.150
It's specifically designed
to have various pieces

00:34:19.150 --> 00:34:21.219
of functionality segmented.

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:25.469
So we have a front
end web application.

00:34:25.469 --> 00:34:27.929
We have a data cache proxy.

00:34:27.929 --> 00:34:31.440
We also have a
computational engine

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:34.949
that just executes
3D volume metrics.

00:34:34.949 --> 00:34:39.239
And the key takeaway
here is that because it's

00:34:39.239 --> 00:34:44.520
delineated you can execute
specific fine-grained access

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:46.880
controls.

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:50.480
The IDgital application itself
runs it in its own project

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:53.989
and it has many
different components

00:34:53.989 --> 00:34:57.370
running in virtual
machines, using Cloud SQL,

00:34:57.370 --> 00:35:00.746
storing all this data in
the transactional database.

00:35:00.746 --> 00:35:04.100
It has its own access
control for front end users,

00:35:04.100 --> 00:35:09.090
and ultimately,
again, is separated

00:35:09.090 --> 00:35:13.660
from the services that access
various parts of the system.

00:35:13.660 --> 00:35:17.640
So this is security
isolation and reducing

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:21.600
that footprint that
might cause challenges

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:24.096
from a security perspective.

00:35:24.096 --> 00:35:26.150
We talked a lot
about the logging.

00:35:26.150 --> 00:35:27.890
Separate project for logging.

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:32.870
Specifically, an
opportunity to segment

00:35:32.870 --> 00:35:35.960
all logs and make them only
available to your compliance

00:35:35.960 --> 00:35:37.440
organization.

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:39.800
Again, HIPPA best practice.

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:45.260
Isolation, ability to transfer
these logs into BigQuery

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:47.070
similar to the clinical data.

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:50.450
Do all kinds of advanced
analytics, anomaly detections.

00:35:50.450 --> 00:35:51.620
Do we have problems?

00:35:51.620 --> 00:35:54.220
Are the wrong people accessing
this data, et cetera,

00:35:54.220 --> 00:35:55.770
et cetera?

00:35:55.770 --> 00:36:00.650
Finally, because this
system is so flexible,

00:36:00.650 --> 00:36:04.340
you have the opportunity to send
your data to various billing

00:36:04.340 --> 00:36:06.990
providers, and the
billing providers,

00:36:06.990 --> 00:36:10.910
of course like everybody else,
don't really share standards.

00:36:10.910 --> 00:36:14.210
So you need to be able to
speak to the specific billing

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:18.080
provider that your customer
has a contract with.

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:19.700
And what are their capabilities?

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:23.750
Native APIs, being able
to send specific billing

00:36:23.750 --> 00:36:27.590
DFTs, the batching, CSVs.

00:36:27.590 --> 00:36:32.450
This again ties into the
microservice architecture.

00:36:32.450 --> 00:36:37.760
Being able to create the right
materialization of your billing

00:36:37.760 --> 00:36:42.350
log and get that sent to the
billing provider in the way

00:36:42.350 --> 00:36:45.710
that they can consume it.

00:36:45.710 --> 00:36:49.460
And again, the system
here is delineated

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:52.110
within its own
microservice architecture.

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:56.100
So with that, just
a quick recap.

00:36:56.100 --> 00:36:57.830
This was a really
very fast walkthrough

00:36:57.830 --> 00:37:00.920
of a complete end-to-end
architecture with zero

00:37:00.920 --> 00:37:02.540
on prem footprint.

00:37:02.540 --> 00:37:05.130
Completely in the cloud.

00:37:05.130 --> 00:37:08.520
Everything is operating the
cloud using many different GCP

00:37:08.520 --> 00:37:09.540
pieces of functionality.

00:37:09.540 --> 00:37:10.800
Everything is automated.

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:13.890
Everything I talked about
here can be automated,

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:15.780
and we're working
continuously to do

00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:19.410
that to create the automation
scripts with IDgital Cloud

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:22.660
Deployment Manager, G
Cloud, Kube control.

00:37:22.660 --> 00:37:25.410
Finally, we leverage
many different components

00:37:25.410 --> 00:37:26.680
of the cloud.

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:28.200
So it's not just Healthcare API.

00:37:28.200 --> 00:37:30.570
You got to bring all the
other pieces together--

00:37:30.570 --> 00:37:33.210
networking, security, BigQuery.

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:35.130
And ultimately
the Healthcare API

00:37:35.130 --> 00:37:38.130
itself is storing this very
critical health care data.

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:41.970
And with that I want to pass
the baton to my colleague

00:37:41.970 --> 00:37:44.897
Patrick, who will tell us
more about the Healthcare API.

00:37:44.897 --> 00:37:45.432
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:45.432 --> 00:37:46.765
PATRICK KLING: Thank you, Sasha.

00:37:50.980 --> 00:37:52.520
Thank you, Sasha.

00:37:52.520 --> 00:37:53.770
So what is the Healthcare API?

00:37:53.770 --> 00:37:56.090
We've heard a lot about it
in Sasha's architecture.

00:37:56.090 --> 00:37:58.330
So fundamentally, the
way we think of it

00:37:58.330 --> 00:38:02.520
is it's a managed scalable and
secure platform for your health

00:38:02.520 --> 00:38:03.560
care data.

00:38:03.560 --> 00:38:07.120
So we built the Healthcare
API to be interoperable,

00:38:07.120 --> 00:38:09.910
so that means it can talk
to your existing systems

00:38:09.910 --> 00:38:11.290
in the health care space.

00:38:11.290 --> 00:38:13.240
And it also supports
industry standards

00:38:13.240 --> 00:38:15.610
that we encounter in
the health care space.

00:38:15.610 --> 00:38:19.030
We've designed it to be
developer friendly and easy

00:38:19.030 --> 00:38:21.230
to use for our customers.

00:38:21.230 --> 00:38:23.260
Of course, security
and compliance

00:38:23.260 --> 00:38:26.240
are very important concerns
in the health care area,

00:38:26.240 --> 00:38:29.200
so we built the Healthcare
API with robust security

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:32.000
and compliance features in mind.

00:38:32.000 --> 00:38:34.060
Finally, the Healthcare
API is designed

00:38:34.060 --> 00:38:36.880
to allow customers to really
unlock the value of the data

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:38.230
that they store in it.

00:38:38.230 --> 00:38:40.000
For example, this is
done by integrating

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:43.250
with powerful analytics and
machine learning solutions.

00:38:43.250 --> 00:38:46.330
So as we heard
earlier in the talk,

00:38:46.330 --> 00:38:48.220
most customers in
the health care space

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:51.040
today have data stored in a
variety of different on prem

00:38:51.040 --> 00:38:52.000
systems.

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:55.040
Typically those are fairly
fragmented or siloed.

00:38:55.040 --> 00:38:57.130
So you'll have
separate data systems

00:38:57.130 --> 00:38:59.440
for different types of data,
different systems maybe

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:01.295
for different
geographical locations.

00:39:01.295 --> 00:39:03.920
You might have different systems
in different parts of a health

00:39:03.920 --> 00:39:05.910
care organization.

00:39:05.910 --> 00:39:08.320
And it's very difficult
for data to travel from one

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:09.890
of those systems to another.

00:39:09.890 --> 00:39:12.010
So what we've done
with the Healthcare API

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:15.040
is we are aiming to
allow our customers

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:18.370
to bring all their data
together in a single place.

00:39:18.370 --> 00:39:20.980
The way we've done
that is by integrating

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:22.070
with existing systems.

00:39:22.070 --> 00:39:24.540
So this includes
packs and VNA systems.

00:39:24.540 --> 00:39:27.040
Those are systems that customers
use to manage their imaging

00:39:27.040 --> 00:39:27.970
data today.

00:39:27.970 --> 00:39:30.040
And also integrating
with EHR systems,

00:39:30.040 --> 00:39:32.932
which have structured
health care records.

00:39:32.932 --> 00:39:34.640
We heard a little bit
about this earlier.

00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:39.310
We support natively a number
of the most common formats

00:39:39.310 --> 00:39:41.510
that we encounter in
the health care area.

00:39:41.510 --> 00:39:44.050
So on the imaging side
this is primarily DICOM.

00:39:44.050 --> 00:39:46.870
So DICOM is a format for
storing medical images and also

00:39:46.870 --> 00:39:49.990
the metadata pertaining
to those images.

00:39:49.990 --> 00:39:55.270
On the electronic health records
side, we support FHIR and HL7.

00:39:55.270 --> 00:39:57.250
We heard a little bit
about the adapters also.

00:39:57.250 --> 00:39:59.020
So these are some
open source components

00:39:59.020 --> 00:40:01.000
that we've put out
there to make it easier

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:03.880
to interact with what we
think of as the legacy

00:40:03.880 --> 00:40:06.850
protocols of exchanging those
types of data, notably DICOM

00:40:06.850 --> 00:40:10.303
[INAUDIBLE] as well as MLLP.

00:40:10.303 --> 00:40:12.220
So what do you mean when
we say the Healthcare

00:40:12.220 --> 00:40:14.440
API is developer friendly?

00:40:14.440 --> 00:40:19.510
Well, like most GCP APIs
it's a modern RESTful API

00:40:19.510 --> 00:40:22.210
built on top of HTTPS and gRPC.

00:40:22.210 --> 00:40:25.630
So the goal for us really was to
allow customers and developers

00:40:25.630 --> 00:40:28.420
to focus on their business
logic while Google would worry

00:40:28.420 --> 00:40:32.050
about things like backups,
configuring VMs, and setting up

00:40:32.050 --> 00:40:33.610
databases.

00:40:33.610 --> 00:40:37.550
The Healthcare API is
a fully serverless API,

00:40:37.550 --> 00:40:40.330
so that means all scaling
and serving infrastructure

00:40:40.330 --> 00:40:43.750
is all handled by
Google's infrastructure.

00:40:43.750 --> 00:40:46.580
We integrated the Healthcare
API with Google Cloud console,

00:40:46.580 --> 00:40:49.240
so this provides our customers
with a graphical user interface

00:40:49.240 --> 00:40:53.440
for easily managing the
resources stored in the API.

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:57.590
Cloud Pub/Sub I think we heard
about it in Sasha's diagrams.

00:40:57.590 --> 00:41:00.220
This is just a very
powerful, versatile mechanism

00:41:00.220 --> 00:41:04.510
for plugging in any kind
of custom processing logic

00:41:04.510 --> 00:41:05.890
into your health care workflow.

00:41:05.890 --> 00:41:08.140
So we think of it as
almost the Swiss army knife

00:41:08.140 --> 00:41:12.130
of integrating with our API.

00:41:12.130 --> 00:41:15.550
Health care data is probably
one of the most sensitive types

00:41:15.550 --> 00:41:17.470
of data out there, and
I'm sure most of you

00:41:17.470 --> 00:41:21.650
will know that privacy is
heavily regulated in the health

00:41:21.650 --> 00:41:23.240
care space.

00:41:23.240 --> 00:41:25.930
So that's why we designed
the Healthcare API

00:41:25.930 --> 00:41:28.990
from the ground up with a
very robust set of security

00:41:28.990 --> 00:41:32.200
and compliance features
built right in.

00:41:32.200 --> 00:41:34.245
Like all of Google
Cloud, the Healthcare API

00:41:34.245 --> 00:41:36.820
is built on top of Google's
trusted infrastructure,

00:41:36.820 --> 00:41:40.990
so it benefits from the
same multilayered security.

00:41:40.990 --> 00:41:43.780
We have built in
fine-grained access controls,

00:41:43.780 --> 00:41:46.000
so this allows customers
to use Cloud IAM

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:48.640
and specify exactly
who gets access to what

00:41:48.640 --> 00:41:50.390
parts of their data.

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:52.390
And then they can also
go to Cloud Audit Logging

00:41:52.390 --> 00:41:55.390
where they can verify who
is actually looking at what

00:41:55.390 --> 00:41:59.800
and maybe detect any anomalies
that might have occurred.

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:01.990
HIPPA compliance is
table stakes when

00:42:01.990 --> 00:42:05.290
handling health care data with
personal information in it.

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:10.540
So we built the Healthcare API
to support HIPPA compliance,

00:42:10.540 --> 00:42:13.630
and we regularly
sign BAA agreements

00:42:13.630 --> 00:42:15.640
with our customers
in this space, which

00:42:15.640 --> 00:42:17.710
is a legal requirement.

00:42:17.710 --> 00:42:20.830
We have also built the
Healthcare API to support data

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:21.490
locality.

00:42:21.490 --> 00:42:24.970
So what this means is a customer
can specify where in the world

00:42:24.970 --> 00:42:27.410
they want their
data to be stored.

00:42:27.410 --> 00:42:29.530
This is important for
health care customers

00:42:29.530 --> 00:42:30.910
in many jurisdictions
where there

00:42:30.910 --> 00:42:36.160
are data residency requirements
that they have to comply with.

00:42:36.160 --> 00:42:39.022
One of the key benefits of
using the Cloud Healthcare API

00:42:39.022 --> 00:42:41.230
is that it allows customers
to bring all their health

00:42:41.230 --> 00:42:44.020
data from where it resides
today and assemble it

00:42:44.020 --> 00:42:47.050
in a single place where they can
reason about it holistically.

00:42:47.050 --> 00:42:48.978
And finally, we also
have some mechanisms

00:42:48.978 --> 00:42:51.020
in the Cloud Healthcare
API that allows customers

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:53.300
to then take those models
and actually deploy them

00:42:53.300 --> 00:42:55.610
into a clinical workflow.

00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:57.380
So for the remainder
of the talk,

00:42:57.380 --> 00:43:01.400
I'd like to walk you through
three key scenarios or three

00:43:01.400 --> 00:43:03.980
key use cases that
we've encountered

00:43:03.980 --> 00:43:05.460
when working in this space.

00:43:05.460 --> 00:43:08.180
So first of all,
I'd like to describe

00:43:08.180 --> 00:43:10.820
how we can support research
and clinical viewing

00:43:10.820 --> 00:43:14.150
of medical images using
the Cloud Healthcare API.

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:16.640
Next, I'll talk a little
bit about high performance

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:18.058
analytics using BigQuery.

00:43:18.058 --> 00:43:19.850
And finally, I'll get
into machine learning

00:43:19.850 --> 00:43:22.940
and how to train and
deploy models there.

00:43:22.940 --> 00:43:26.900
So the Healthcare API
is a flexible platform

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:29.960
for viewing medical
imaging data.

00:43:29.960 --> 00:43:33.290
Images can be ingested
into the Healthcare API

00:43:33.290 --> 00:43:34.903
either from a PACS
system directly--

00:43:34.903 --> 00:43:36.320
so this is kind
of similar to what

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:38.750
we saw in some of the
architecture diagrams

00:43:38.750 --> 00:43:42.050
previously where we have images
traveling from an on prem PACS

00:43:42.050 --> 00:43:44.060
and into the
Healthcare API maybe

00:43:44.060 --> 00:43:46.420
using one of our adapters.

00:43:46.420 --> 00:43:49.843
But we also support bulk import
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:43:49.843 --> 00:43:51.760
And the nice thing about
the bulk import piece

00:43:51.760 --> 00:43:53.885
is that it actually enables
customers to use things

00:43:53.885 --> 00:43:55.100
like the Transfer Appliance.

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:56.750
So this is a tool
that allows customers

00:43:56.750 --> 00:43:59.750
to do bulk loads of very
large amounts of data

00:43:59.750 --> 00:44:01.940
that would maybe take a
very long time to send over

00:44:01.940 --> 00:44:02.582
the network.

00:44:02.582 --> 00:44:04.040
With the Transfer
Appliance, we can

00:44:04.040 --> 00:44:07.970
bypass that and get it into the
Healthcare API more quickly.

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:10.400
We have then worked with
a number of partners

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:14.550
to build Healthcare API support
into medical imaging viewers.

00:44:14.550 --> 00:44:17.030
So we already heard about
one of them, eUnity.

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:19.790
There is another
commercial viewer, IMS,

00:44:19.790 --> 00:44:22.490
that's also supporting
the Cloud Healthcare API.

00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:24.990
But we've also worked with a
number of open source projects.

00:44:24.990 --> 00:44:28.220
So WEASIS and OHIF are a
couple of open source viewers

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:30.560
that also can talk to the
Healthcare API directly.

00:44:30.560 --> 00:44:32.810
So for all of these they can
fetch the images directly

00:44:32.810 --> 00:44:35.780
from the API, so it's a truly
serverless, end-to-end viewing

00:44:35.780 --> 00:44:38.280
experience.

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:40.060
On the analytic
side of the story,

00:44:40.060 --> 00:44:41.940
we rely heavily on BigQuery.

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:45.960
So BigQuery is Google's
petabyte scale, high performance

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:46.733
analytics engine.

00:44:46.733 --> 00:44:48.150
It's something we
built originally

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:50.040
for our own internal
use at Google

00:44:50.040 --> 00:44:52.590
to understand user behavior
on some of our consumer

00:44:52.590 --> 00:44:54.120
facing products.

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:56.580
So via the Cloud
Healthcare API, we're

00:44:56.580 --> 00:44:59.193
making this powerful tool
available to our customers

00:44:59.193 --> 00:45:01.110
in the health care space
by allowing customers

00:45:01.110 --> 00:45:03.958
to load their health
data into BigQuery.

00:45:03.958 --> 00:45:05.375
Once their data
is in BigQuery, it

00:45:05.375 --> 00:45:08.640
becomes very easy to join across
different data modalities,

00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:11.770
join in additional data sets,
maybe join in some public data

00:45:11.770 --> 00:45:12.270
sets.

00:45:12.270 --> 00:45:15.320
We have some of those
available, like the RxNorm data

00:45:15.320 --> 00:45:17.548
set, which is a
prescription drug database.

00:45:17.548 --> 00:45:19.590
So you can maybe combine
your health care records

00:45:19.590 --> 00:45:22.560
data, your imaging metadata,
and maybe some genomics data,

00:45:22.560 --> 00:45:24.870
and then you can really
start to reason about those.

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:29.230
So there is a nearly endless
lists of use cases for this,

00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:31.895
so I'm highlighting
three of them here.

00:45:31.895 --> 00:45:34.020
So we've got some customers
that are using BigQuery

00:45:34.020 --> 00:45:38.680
to identify and select cohorts
to include in research studies.

00:45:38.680 --> 00:45:41.287
So select a list of
patients, for instance,

00:45:41.287 --> 00:45:42.870
and then maybe
materialize their data,

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:46.110
deidentify it, and hand
it over to researchers.

00:45:46.110 --> 00:45:48.780
We've also seen customers using
BigQuery for clinical decision

00:45:48.780 --> 00:45:49.300
support.

00:45:49.300 --> 00:45:51.750
So this is an example
where maybe we

00:45:51.750 --> 00:45:54.330
want to find similar
patients to the one

00:45:54.330 --> 00:45:57.250
that a provider is
currently looking at.

00:45:57.250 --> 00:45:59.970
And finally, because
BigQuery is fast

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:01.530
it allows fast
turnaround on queries.

00:46:01.530 --> 00:46:03.450
So it's a very useful
tool for iterating

00:46:03.450 --> 00:46:05.473
on things like quality
and performance metrics,

00:46:05.473 --> 00:46:07.890
where we want to start measuring
things in the health care

00:46:07.890 --> 00:46:09.990
enterprise that
are very hard to do

00:46:09.990 --> 00:46:13.547
in the existing on prem silos.

00:46:13.547 --> 00:46:15.880
Finally, let's talk a little
bit about machine learning.

00:46:15.880 --> 00:46:18.900
So one of the key benefits
of the Cloud Healthcare API

00:46:18.900 --> 00:46:22.110
is that it can bring your health
care data to advanced machine

00:46:22.110 --> 00:46:22.980
learning solutions.

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:24.480
So these machine
learning solutions,

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:26.790
again, can take advantage of
the full spectrum of health care

00:46:26.790 --> 00:46:27.480
data--

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:29.880
health care records,
imaging data, genomics data,

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:31.500
you name it.

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:34.240
We integrate with some
powerful labeling tools.

00:46:34.240 --> 00:46:36.120
So those can be
used by customers

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:40.620
to curate high quality training
data sets for their machine

00:46:40.620 --> 00:46:41.900
learning use cases.

00:46:41.900 --> 00:46:43.650
And then we can make
those data and labels

00:46:43.650 --> 00:46:46.260
available to a Cloud
Machine Learning Engine

00:46:46.260 --> 00:46:49.350
and AutoML, which are some of
the advanced machine learning

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:51.750
tools built into
Google Cloud Platform.

00:46:51.750 --> 00:46:53.250
So Cloud Machine
Learning Engine you

00:46:53.250 --> 00:46:55.770
can think of it as our
hosted version of TensorFlow.

00:46:55.770 --> 00:46:58.590
So this is something that allows
serverless training of machine

00:46:58.590 --> 00:46:59.910
learning models.

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:03.150
AutoML is a tool that's
specialized for customers that

00:47:03.150 --> 00:47:05.370
may not have a lot of
data science or machine

00:47:05.370 --> 00:47:07.900
learning expertise
at their disposal.

00:47:07.900 --> 00:47:11.190
And it's a powerful tool that
can allow customers to train

00:47:11.190 --> 00:47:14.040
models even in those contexts.

00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:16.290
Finally, once a customer
ha trained a model,

00:47:16.290 --> 00:47:18.150
the Healthcare API can
also help them deploy

00:47:18.150 --> 00:47:19.670
this model back
into their workflow.

00:47:19.670 --> 00:47:21.930
So for example, if
I have a model that

00:47:21.930 --> 00:47:24.150
can help me understand
an x-ray image,

00:47:24.150 --> 00:47:26.700
I can use the Cloud Healthcare
API to bring the prediction

00:47:26.700 --> 00:47:29.440
from that model back into
the existing PACS viewer

00:47:29.440 --> 00:47:31.110
so a radiologist
can view the model

00:47:31.110 --> 00:47:35.970
prediction alongside the images
when they're doing their read.

00:47:35.970 --> 00:47:38.550
So one thing I would
like to announce

00:47:38.550 --> 00:47:40.590
is that the Cloud Healthcare
API as of last week

00:47:40.590 --> 00:47:42.222
is actually available
in public beta,

00:47:42.222 --> 00:47:43.680
so this means it's
something that's

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:47.950
now available to all Google
Cloud customers to use.

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:51.020
We also have some
public imaging data

00:47:51.020 --> 00:47:54.150
sets available hosted on the
Cloud Healthcare API already,

00:47:54.150 --> 00:47:56.040
so this can be a
great place for you

00:47:56.040 --> 00:47:58.470
to start playing with some
of our viewer integrations

00:47:58.470 --> 00:48:01.680
and maybe start trying
out some of the tools

00:48:01.680 --> 00:48:02.740
that we discussed today.

00:48:02.740 --> 00:48:05.590
So notable we have the NIH
chest x-ray data set on there,

00:48:05.590 --> 00:48:08.500
and we also have the cancer
imaging archive on there.

00:48:08.500 --> 00:48:09.000
All right.

00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:11.458
So with that, I'd like to thank
you all for your attention.

00:48:11.458 --> 00:48:14.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

